Date: Sun, 9 May 93 14:21:29 PDT
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: AI:Human Equivalence
X-Reposting-Policy: Redistribute freely

It's already true that we have the hardware needed to produce human-
equivalent AI (if it is possible at all).  Our hardware can do anything
the hardware of the future can; ours is just slower.  (At worst we need
more disk drives.)

The point is, it doesn't matter how fast your hardware is if you don't
know what program to run on it.  The real problem isn't the hardware, it's
the software.  Extrapolating current trends in computer speedups is both
unsound (the same methodology would have failed during almost any
innovative period in the past, giving us, for example, locomotives
travelling at thousands of miles per hour, or cotton picking equipment
capable of clearing a whole field in milliseconds) and pointless, as
faster computers are neither necessary nor sufficient for AI.

Skeptically,

Hal Finney
hal@alumni.caltech.edu

