There are degree's of reflexes, from the simple reflex arcs such as the
knee-jerk response which are mediated via neurons in the spinal cord to
the more complex reflexes which involve cortical neurons.  Suckling is
a more complex reflex than the knee-jerk.  The plain fact of the matter
is that there is no hard and fast distinction between reflex arcs and
cognition loops.  Reflexes become more complex as higher parts of the
spinal cord and then cortex are drawn into the loop.  A sufficiently
perverse behaviourist could describe my actions at the keyboard in terms
of conditioned reflexes, but it wouldn't be a useful definition.

> Now it's your turn to really employ a straw man.  Man, that is, the
> human species, is the rational animal, that is, the only animal known
> to have the survival tool of reason.

Utter rubbish.  Many higher animals employ reason.  Chimps have complex
social structures and use language as a means to lie and deceive each
other, forming teams and operating tit-for-tat alliances to climb the
troop hierarchy.  By reflex?  Anyone who has observed the west african
forest chimps hunt monkeys knows there's lot going on inside the skull
of chimps that can't possibly be described as reflexive.  One of the
theories for the rapid evolution of the human brain is that it evolved
not to cope with inter-species environmental hazards but from intra-
species hierachial pressures.  Outwit the troop leader, become the alpha
male, and you had more children.  So the concept of other people having
mental states is seen as cruical to the evolution of the human brain. 
That such a key concept is hard-wired in surprises me not the least.

The fact is that the reasoning abilities of animals lie along a spectrum
ourselves at one end (with the apes, dolphins, elephants, whales etc
fairly close).  Paint a chimp's and baboon's facial cheeks and give them
mirrors.  A chimp will realise (after a while) that the image in the
mirror is itself and try to remove the paint that it *reasons* (but
can't directly observe) is on it's face.  A baboon, being a creature of
reduced reasoning powers (compared to Bonzo) will not make this
connection.  Then there are the classic experiments where chimps stack
up crates to reach a banana otherwise out of reach.  Are they doing that
by reflex?  No, they are using reason and logic.  Similarly when a dog
digs a tunnel under a fence to break into a chicken pen.  Is that a
reflex action?  No, it's called intelligent behaviour.

