Date: Sun, 9 May 93 22:53:25 PDT
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: CRYONICS: Reviving a neuro suspendee
X-Reposting-Policy: Redistribute freely

I have seen three different scenarios discussed for how a body may
be created and attached to the head of someone who takes on neurosuspension.
In addition, there is a fourth scenario, uploading, in which the head
never acquires a body but comes to life as a computer simulation in a
virtual reality.

Thomas Donaldson on Cryonet suggested that a body would actually be
grown from the base of the head, being attached to the neck at all times.
I don't really see how this would work, as it's difficult for me to
imagine an embryonic (using the term figuratively) spinal cord, nervous
and digestive system actually being attached to the mature counterparts
in the neck at all times in the development process.  Our DNA codes for
development of the body in a certain order, and I don't think you can
change that order freely.  Some animals can re-grow limbs, though, so
perhaps this process could serve as a model for what we want.

A more commonly imagined possibility is to clone one or more of the
existing cells and produce a new body, perhaps through a rather natural
process similar to that which we have all gone through.  The head of this
new body would be removed (presumably brain growth was suppressed) and
the revived head attached.  It is hoped that due to the body being a
clone that it will be close to identical to the original body and
therefore the attachment process will be not too difficult.

I think there are a lot of problems with this idea, too, starting with
the problems with growing a physically mature body which nevertheless
lacks a brain, and progressing to the many differences which exist
between identical twins, especially as mature adults.  I wouldn't expect
the topology of the nervous system to match between the brain and the
cloned body, and I don't see how you would acquire the information needed
to match up these incompatible halves.

A third possibility is a variant on this; you simulate the growth of a
cloned body on a computer (presumably running a simulation at the
cellular or sub-cellular level, but probably not at the molecular level),
figure out what the body would or should look like, then tell your
nanomachines to build it in whatever order they like.  Then you attach
the head.  This bypasses the step of actually growing a clone and waiting
20 years for it to reach adulthood, although you are still likely to have
matchup problems when you attach the head.

One of the things I like about whole-body is that you don't have this
matchup problem.  You've got a body right there that you know has nerve
trunks which match what the brain expects.  If you grow a clone you might
have many percent more or fewer nerve trunks than you need, they may be
dispersed and distributed differently, and it may not be at all clear how
to hook them up.

I think you'll have the same problem if you try to upload.  Uploading a
person will require giving him a simulated body which matches the nerve
connections of his simulated brain.  If you start with an intact body
you can trace each nerve and say, this one needs to go to the foot,
this to the hand, and so on.  But if all you had were a brain, you
don't have that information.

Now, you may say, can't you just trace the nerves up into the brain, see
where they go and how they are connected, and from that DEDUCE where they
must have attached to in the body?

I don't think this will be as easy as it sounds (and I know that it
doesn't sound very easy!).  The brain is an active device.  Knowledge of
its pattern of interconnects will not in and of itself tell you
everything there is to know about how it behaves.  It may be necessary to
actually simulate the operation of the brain in order to find out what it
would do with certain patterns of inputs.

It's like a computer for which you are given a computer program.  You are
asked some question about how the computer will behave when it runs
this program.  One famous one is, will this program ever halt or will
it run forever?  For some simple programs and questions you can give an
answer.  But for most cases you just have to run the program and see what
it does.

That's what I'm afraid is likely to be the case with the brain.  Unless
it turns out to be startlingly simple, it may be that the only way to
determine whether THIS neuron is attached to the top of the big toe or a
spot a millimeter away is to run the brain under both cases and see whether
it notices any differences between this input and its memories of how its
toes used to feel.

If this process is necessary, then given the many inputs to the brain,
there may be a combinatorial explosion of possibilities to try to pin
down.  Until you are close to right the match-to-memory process can't
work - the brain will just experience a confusing jumble of sensations
that doesn't correspond at all with its memories.  And there would be so
many possibilities that the universe's lifetime is not enough to try out
all the possibilities.

(Consider this thought experiment.  I train a present-day neural net which
takes its inputs from an irregularly spaced 8-by-8 array to recognize
the letters of the alphabet.  This has 64 inputs, say 15 hidden-layer
neurons, and 26 output-layer neurons.  It will have 1,350 weights on its 
15+26=41 neurons.  Now I remove the information about which input went
where, and build you a new "retina" with 70 differently-positioned
neurons.  Your task is to connect this "cloned" (inaccurately) retina to
the neural network in such a way as to reproduce, as closely as possible,
the neuron activations which produced letter recognition before.  I'd
suspect that trial and error is going to be the best way to go, and I'd
be surprised if you could reproduce anything similar to the original
network.)

Not only is there the computational problem, there is the ethical issue
of subjecting a concious mind to these experiences.  Even if the brain is
"just" a simulation, if you are matching sensations to memories then I'd
argue that there's someone experiencing these sensations and having these
memories.  Given the tedium involved in this process, which could go on
for subjective years even if somehow it is sped up in realtime, this
could be considered a form of torture.  All memory of this could be
erased afterwards, of course, but based on earlier discussion on this
list I would argue that this in no way eliminates the ethical impact of
subjecting a human being to such an experience, nor does it make me as a
potential suspendee willing to put myself in that position.

Hal Finney
hal@alumni.caltech.edu

