Karl Barrus writes:

> Hm... I wonder if this will lead to new neural networks which output
> imaginary values, or ones which have imaginary weights.  This would
> allow magnitude and phase information!

I just recently read the following article:

  Michael C Mozer, Richard S Zemel, Marlene Behrman, and Christopher
  K I Williams.  Learning to Segment Images Using Dynamic Feature
  Binding.  Neural Computation 4 (1992), pp 650-665.

In this paper, they have complex-valued activations and weights
(and use a generalization of backprop).  In their system, the
numbers are actually expressed in polar coordinates; the "phase"
is then used to represent a labeling.  The idea is to have the
network dynamically relax onto consistent labelings by matching
the phases of units firing in response to features from the same
object (well, rectangle or triangle...).

Interesting paper.  There was no claim, however, that biological
nets use phase for this particular purpose.

derek


